WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.610
Today AI reads long policies and meeting notes with you, and you learn to check what it says.

2
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:13.300
Attach the document with the paperclip or + button, then say who the summary is for and why.

3
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:21.020
“A one-page summary for new staff: what changes, deadlines and amounts” beats “summarise this”.

4
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.130
Ask it to mention the section for each point.

5
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:30.880
You can ask, “What is the last date to submit a travel claim?” and get an answer in seconds.

6
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.160
Ask it to quote the exact line and the section number.

7
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.840
If the answer is not in the document, tell it to say so instead of guessing.

8
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.350
NotebookLM is a free Google tool that answers only from the files you add, called sources.

9
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.650
Each answer has small numbered links that jump to the exact lines it used.

10
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:59.560
It is ideal for policies, manuals and study material.

11
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.200
Paste rough meeting notes and ask for a table: task, owner, deadline.

12
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.450
If a task has no owner or date, ask the AI to mark it as missing.

13
00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.680
Then check every name and date before sharing.

14
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:16.460
Both Claude and ChatGPT let you attach a file with the paperclip or plus button.

15
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:22.860
Say who the summary is for, how long it should be, and ask for section numbers.

16
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.750
Those section numbers are your shortcut for checking the original quickly.

17
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:34.300
We attach a twelve-section practice policy and ask for a summary for new staff.

18
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:41.310
Claude gives short points, each with its section number, so checking takes minutes, not hours.

19
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:47.810
Now a quick question about the same document in ChatGPT.

20
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.420
The answer says thirty days, which sounds believable.

21
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:55.000
We open section six point one, and the policy actually says fifteen days.

22
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.840
One wrong number like this could cost someone their money, so we always check.

23
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.470
NotebookLM answers only from the sources you add to a notebook.

24
00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:12.080
Each answer has small numbered markers, and clicking one shows the exact lines it used.

25
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.470
That makes checking very fast, but you still read those lines yourself.

26
00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:24.040
Meeting notes are messy, so we ask for a clean table of task, owner and deadline.

27
00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.230
Gemini marks the gaps as missing instead of inventing a name or date.

28
00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:35.860
Now you know exactly what to ask in the team group before the work starts.

29
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.290
Great work! Let’s remember what we learned.

30
00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:43.170
Now build along, then try the challenges. You’ve got this!
